The fix does what #1247 asked: a hook that writes more than ~64 KiB to stderr no longer deadlocks until the 5-second timeout.

• The fix: ShellHook::run_command now reads the hook's stderr on a separate thread while it runs, instead of only after it exits. Memory stays bounded at the same 4096-byte head and 4096-byte tail as before, and the cut is marked in the same words, which both code paths now share.
• Tests I ran: the 4 new tests in src/hooks_stderr_drain_tests.rs pass in 0.10s with cargo test --bin yoyo hooks_stderr_drain. They cover 200 KB of stderr (finishes fast, capped, head and tail markers kept), a small stderr passed through unchanged, output exactly at the cap, and a multi-byte character crossing the cut.
• No leftover sabotage: grep -c NEUTERED src/hooks.rs returns 0.
• One deviation, accepted: after a timeout kill, the code doesn't wait for the reader thread to finish. The comment explains why: a process the hook started in the background can keep the pipe open. After a normal exit, the wait has a time limit.
• Touchpoints: every new helper and module is used in this diff. The existing check that run_command applies the cap was updated to look for the new call, and the size limit for src/hooks.rs in tests/module_size.rs now matches its 3780 lines and has a dated note.
• Docs: none needed. docs/src says nothing about limits on hook output.

